The following are the figures and tables:

Figure 1. Mean proportion of bored internodes and mean percentage of insect survival (larvae, pupae and adults) for the discovery population. Data were analyzed as proportion with binomial, but they are indicated in percentage. The figure highlights the susceptible S58 check, and the resistant checks S3 and S59.
Figure 2. Mean proportion of bored internodes and mean percentage of insect survival (larvae, pupae and adults) for the validation population. Data were analyzed as proportion with binomial, but they are indicated in percentage. The figure highlights the susceptible and resistant checks S58 and S3.
Figure 3. Resistant sugarcane genotypes identified based on the damage-survival index (DSI) response to Diatraea spp. The figure shows the 51 genotypes classified as resistant, with the selected genotypes highlighted for the use within the plant breeding program.
Figure 4. Structure analysis of 519 sugarcane genotypes from the core panel at Cenicaña, based on 121,166 SNPs. Subpopulations 1, 2, 3, and 4 are represented by pink, green, cyan, and blue colors respectively. Red dots above the genotype represent the subset of 348 samples used in this study.
Figure 5. Manhattan and Quantile-Quantile plots for the damage survival index for the general model. The X-axis in the Manhattan plot corresponds to the chromosomes, with CS representing the collection of contigs and scaffolds, and the Y-axis shows values of -log10(p-value). The green line in the Manhattan plot represents the significance threshold. 
Figure 6. Manhattan and Quantile-Quantile plots for the damage survival index for the 5-dom-alt model. The X-axis in the Manhattan plot corresponds to the chromosomes, with CS representing the collection of contigs and scaffolds, and the Y-axis shows values of -log10(p-value). The green line in the Manhattan plot represents the significance threshold.
Figure 7. Manhattan and Quantile-Quantile plots for the damage survival index for the 3-dom-alt model. The X-axis in the Manhattan plot corresponds to the chromosomes, with CS representing the collection of contigs and scaffolds, and the Y-axis shows values of -log10(p-value). The green line in the Manhattan plot represents the significance threshold.
Figure 8. KEGG pathway enrichment analysis of candidate genes associated with resistance to Diatraea spp. Enriched pathways are shown according to their statistical significance, highlighting biological processes related to primary energy metabolism, respiratory metabolism, and defense-associated metabolic responses.


Table S1. List of the 519 genotypes from the core panel maintained at CENICAÑA. The table includes the subset of 220 genotypes from the discovery population, the 128 elite parents from the validation population, and the remaining genotypes from the core panel not analyzed in this study.
Table S2. Chromosome-specific LD decay distances estimated at the r² = 0.1 threshold. Distances are reported in kilobases (kb).
Table S3. Phenotypic values of the proportion of bored internode (BI), the proportion of insect survival (SInd), and the damage-survival index (DSI) for each genotype from the discover and validation population.
Table S4. Functional categories of candidate genes identified within the LD decay window of 353.1 Kb around the validated markers associated with resistance to Diatraea spp. The table physical distance between each candidate gene and the validated marker, where negative distance values indicate candidate genes located downstream of the associated marker.
Figure S1. Chromosome-specific linkage disequilibrium (LD) decay curves. Pairwise r² values are plotted against physical distance (kb) for each chromosome. The LOESS-fitted curve (black line) represents the LD decay pattern, the horizontal red line indicates the r² = 0.1 threshold, and the vertical green line marks the estimated LD decay distance for each chromosome.
Figure S2. Quantile–quantile (QQ) plots for the twelve genetic models evaluated for resistance to Diatraea spp. Each panel compares the observed and expected −log₁₀(p) values to assess the distribution of association signals and the adequacy of population structure and relatedness correction in each model.
